CHAPTERS = [
  CH("Foundations", "vit", "Grape Growing — Viticulture", """
<p>Nearly all wine of consequence comes from one species, <em>Vitis vinifera</em>, and most vines of it stand on roots borrowed from American species. The aphid phylloxera feeds on vinifera roots and American vines tolerate it, so grafting is the settled answer and a vineyard is two plants joined at the ground. Deep sand defeats the louse, though, and it never reached some regions, so Chile, South Australia and parts of Washington and Argentina are still own-rooted.</p>

<h4>The vine's year</h4>
<p>${INTRO_CHIP('budbreak')}${INTRO_CHIP('flowering')}${INTRO_CHIP('fruit set')}${INTRO_CHIP('veraison')}${INTRO_CHIP('harvest')}</p>
<p>Fruit set decides how many berries the vine carries, so a cold wet flowering costs volume no later sunshine gives back. Veraison is the turn from growing to ripening: berries soften and colour, sugar climbs, acidity falls. Picking is a judgement about where those lines have reached. Weather can settle it sooner: frost after budbreak kills the shoots carrying the crop, hail strips a slope in minutes, and rain before harvest swells the berries and lets rot into damp bunches.</p>

<h4>Site, and what a grower does with it</h4>
<p>Most vineyards lie roughly 30 to 50 degrees from the equator, where a season runs warm enough to ripen fruit and cool enough to leave it fresh. Inside that band the detail does the work. A slope angled at the sun takes light nearer head-on and warms faster. Cold nights slow the vine's burning of acid, so altitude is prized in hot places. Poor, free-draining ground keeps a vine hungry and its berries small; rich wet soil buys leafy shoots and dilute fruit.</p>
<p>The labour follows. Winter pruning sets the crop before a leaf appears, by long canes or short spurs. In summer, shoots are positioned and leaves pulled so light reaches the fruit and air moves through the bunches, ripening skins and holding down mildew. The same fungus, <i>Botrytis cinerea</i>, is ruinous grey rot on wet or damaged fruit and prized noble rot on healthy ripe whites.</p>
"""),

  CH("Foundations", "vin", "Winemaking — Vinification", """
<p>One reaction sits under all of it: yeast consume grape sugar and give back alcohol and carbon dioxide. Every other cellar decision is about what the juice touches on the way, and when.</p>

<h4>Red, white and pink</h4>
<p>Pulp is nearly colourless in almost every variety, so colour and tannin must come from the skins. White grapes are pressed first and the juice ferments alone, kept cool because warmth drives delicate aromas off. Red grapes ferment with their skins, warmer, and pressing comes afterwards. Carbon dioxide floats those skins into a cap, worked by punching down or pumping over ${INTRO_CHIP('pigeage')}${INTRO_CHIP('remontage')} so extraction continues and the crust never dries and sours. Rose sits between, from brief skin contact or from juice bled off a red vat early.</p>

<h4>After the sugar has gone</h4>
<p>Malolactic conversion is bacterial rather than yeast work, turning sharp malic acid into softer lactic acid. It is routine for reds, a stylistic choice for whites. Wine left on its spent yeast, or stirred through it, picks up weight and a savoury, bready character. Oak supplies flavour and a slow trickle of air: a small new barrel marks a wine heavily, a large old cask hardly at all, because what counts is how much wood touches how much wine and how often it has been filled.</p>
<p>The sweet and the strong styles come of interrupting, concentrating or fortifying. With spirit, timing decides. Add it while sugar remains and the ferment stops with sweetness intact, as in Port and the Vins Doux Naturels. Add it to a wine already fermented dry and no sweetness follows, as in Fino and Oloroso Sherry, where the strength chosen decides whether flor lives or dies. Concentrate the sugar first, by drying grapes, freezing them or letting noble rot dehydrate them, and the yeast give up before they finish. Trap the gas of a second fermentation, in bottle or sealed tank, and the wine sparkles.</p>
"""),
]
